course: language
lesson: 29
title: Sarcasm and tone of voice
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Sarcasm is saying the opposite of what you mean, usually to show you're annoyed or to tease; the words say one thing and the situation and voice say another.
explain: The family steps out for the beach, the sky opens, and Dad says flatly "Oh, great. Perfect beach weather"; the rain, his face and his tired voice tell you he is not happy at all.
explain: Tone is the feeling in your voice: a full name and a sharp "NOW!" can say "you're in trouble" louder than any words.
explain: In texts there is no voice and no face, so sarcasm is easy to misread; that is why people add emoji or tone tags like "/s" for sarcastic and "/j" for joking.
explain: Sarcasm can sting, and some people (including many learners of English and some autistic people) find it harder to spot, so when it matters, say plainly what you mean.
fact: Sarcasm means saying the opposite of what you mean, to mock something or show annoyance; tone is the feeling in your voice.
fact: To spot sarcasm, check whether the words fit the situation and the tone; if they don't fit, it may be sarcasm.
fact: A sarcastic "Thanks a LOT" means "I'm annoyed with you"; saying sorry works better than replying "You're welcome!".
fact: In texts there is no voice or face, so sarcasm is easily misunderstood; tone tags like "/s" (sarcastic) and "/j" (joking) or emoji can help.
fact: In Scotland "Aye, right!" usually means "I don't believe you", even though "aye" means yes and "right" means correct.
fact: Teachers sometimes use sarcasm to tease gently, but it can hurt, and many teachers now avoid it with children.
fact: Using a child's full name and a sharp voice shows that a parent is serious or cross.
fact: A silly impossible add-on like "Yeah, sure, and a pony too" means "no".
fact: "She'll be right" in Australia and New Zealand means "it'll be fine, don't worry"; "she" means the situation, not a person, and "yeah, nah" often means "no" or "well, not really".
fact: A flat, bored voice with excited words is a sign of sarcasm; some people find sarcasm harder to read, so explain kindly if they miss it.
fact: Sarcasm exists in many languages: in Ukrainian "Чудово!" (wonderful) said in a flat, tired voice is sarcastic too.
exercise: It pours as the family leaves for the beach. Dad says "Perfect beach weather." What does he mean? | He's annoyed that it's raining | He loves swimming in the rain and can't wait to go | The weather is really perfect | He wants to stay longer
exercise: What is sarcasm? | Saying the opposite of what you mean to mock or show annoyance | Saying exactly what you mean, very politely, to someone you've just met | Telling a long funny story | Speaking in a very quiet voice
exercise: Mia spills juice on her brother's homework. He says "Thanks a LOT." What's the best reply? | Sorry, I'll help fix it | You're welcome! | No problem at all | Any time!
exercise: Ben texts "nice one" after his friend forgets the football. Why can't the friend tell if Ben is angry? | Texts have no voice or face to show tone | Ben spelled it wrongly | "Nice one" always means angry, in every single text | His phone was broken
exercise: What does the tone tag "/s" mean in a text? | I'm being sarcastic | I'm being serious | Send it again | Sorry
exercise: A Glasgow boy says "Aye, right!" to his friend's shark story. What does he mean? | I don't believe you | Yes, that's correct | Turn right | Yes, I saw it too
exercise: Tom's teacher says "Well done for being on time" when he's 20 minutes late. What is she doing? | Teasing him with sarcasm | Praising him sincerely for trying hard | Mixing up the time | Giving him a prize
exercise: Mum says "Hannah Elizabeth Smith, come here NOW!" What does Hannah understand? | She's in trouble | Mum is happy to see her | Mum forgot her name | It's her birthday
exercise: Ruby asks for a sweet. Mum says "Yeah, sure, and a pony too." What's the answer? | No | Yes, a sweet and a pony | Yes, but only a pony | Ask Dad
exercise: Kai's Aussie mate says "She'll be right" when the tent falls down. What does he mean? | It'll be fine, don't worry | A girl will fix it | The tent is right | Someone called "she" is coming
exercise: Lucy says "I'm so excited for my maths test" in a flat voice. What gives away the sarcasm? | The flat voice doesn't match the excited words | The word "maths", which is always said sarcastically | She said it at school | She said "so"
exercise: Ellie's dad's Ukrainian friend says "Чудово!" in a flat tired voice about a bill. What is he doing? | Being sarcastic, like "Oh brilliant" | Saying the bill is wonderful and cheap | Saying thank you | Counting money
exercise: Why might some people find sarcasm harder to spot? | They may take the words literally | They are not listening to you on purpose because they're bored | They are being rude | Sarcasm is only in Scotland
exercise: Which is a sign that someone might be sarcastic? | The words don't fit the situation | They speak in a full sentence | They smile and mean it | They use long words
exercise: What does "/j" mean as a tone tag? | I'm joking | I'm jealous | Just now | Join me
test: Your mate goes "people always mean what they say, bruv, words are words." What's true? | Sometimes the situation and tone show the opposite meaning | He's right, words always mean exactly what they say, whatever the voice sounds like | Only in films do people use sarcasm | Only adults mean what they say
test: Your London cousin hears "Aye, right" from a Glasgow lad and thinks he believes the shark story. What's really meant? | He doesn't believe it | He totally believes it | He wants to go right | He's scared of sharks
test: What would happen if you texted "great, thanks a lot" to a friend with no emoji after they forgot your birthday? | They might not know if you're cross or happy | They'd always know you're joking | The phone would automatically add a sad face to it | Nothing, texts always show tone
test: A Gen Z cousin texts "omg ur SO good at timing 💀 /s" after you're late. ("💀" here means I'm dying of laughter.) What does she mean? | She's teasing you about being late | She really thinks you're always on time | She's scared of skulls | She's angry and will never talk to you
test: Your nan from Wales says "lush weather for a picnic, isn't it" as hail bounces off the car. ("Lush" means lovely.) What does she mean? | The weather is awful for a picnic | The weather is really lovely for a picnic | She loves hail | The picnic is ready
test: Ruby asks "where's the pony then?" after Mum said "and a pony too". What should Ruby understand? | It was a sarcastic way of saying no | Mum ordered a pony | The pony is outside | Mum will buy the pony later
test: Your dad, a big fan of 80s films, says "Oh, this is gonna be RAD" in a flat voice as the car breaks down. ("Rad" means great, 1980s slang.) Is he happy? | No, he's being sarcastic | Yes, he loves broken cars | Yes, he's going to fix it with joy | He's talking about a film
test: A cartoon character says "Oh WONDERFUL" in a happy voice with a big smile after winning a prize. Is it sarcasm? | No, the words, voice and situation all fit | Yes, "wonderful" is always sarcastic in cartoons | Yes, cartoons are always sarcastic | It's impossible to tell
test: Your mate gets upset when you say "nice drawing, NOT" as a joke. What's the wise move? | Say sorry and say what you really meant kindly | Tell him he can't take a joke and should toughen up | Say it louder | Ignore him
test: Kai's Aussie mate says "yeah, nah, we're not camping again." What does "yeah, nah" mean here? | No | Yes | Maybe tomorrow | Definitely yes
test: A teacher says "Lovely, Tom, very helpful" when Tom knocks over the paints. What is she showing? | She's annoyed and teasing | She is truly pleased | She wants more paint spilled | She is giving Tom a reward
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises sarcasm? | The utterance of words contrary to the intended meaning, so as to convey mockery or displeasure | The utterance of words entirely consistent with the intended meaning, in a courteous manner | The use of exceptionally formal vocabulary | The practice of speaking in a whisper
exam: Identify the principal reason for which sarcasm is frequently misapprehended in written text messages. | The absence of vocal tone and facial expression | The excessive length of such messages | The prohibition of punctuation in such messages | The use of capital letters at the start of sentences
exam: In respect of the Scottish expression "Aye, right", select the response consistent with the established facts. | It ordinarily signifies disbelief notwithstanding its two affirmative words | It ordinarily signifies enthusiastic agreement | It constitutes a direction to turn right | It is regarded as an error of grammar
exam: Determine the meaning of the reply "Yeah, sure, and a pony too" to a request for a sweet. | A refusal | An unconditional consent | A promise of two gifts | A request for clarification
